# Pong

## Sound API
- play PCM embedded data pointed to by file wrapper
ac97.c: `void play(struct file pcm_data);`

# Scheduler
- have fork() continue exiting from where the parent called instead of
giving it an entry* function to start from

- add boost values to MLFQ (whoops not completely implemented yet) and use OSTEP
hw for sanity checking values.

- only demote a process' level in the MLFQ if it completely used up its time slice (
    global boolean, unset by process and set by timer interrupt, checked by scheduler?
    should be simple eks dee).

- add TSS tracking for privilege levels through different stacks (similarly,
add privilege level to type in vma region describing stack per process)

- make cpu.{c,h} and move struct cpu, read_rflags() into it

- look up how gcc handles private function calling sequences, and determine if
forkret() [process.c] can be made private (static).

- properly handle exiting processes. this includes reaping ZOMBIE processes and freeing
their memory, as well as the slots that they take up in the process table. careful that
this is done carefully around the scheduler code that is very prone to mistakes!

- verify guard page for stacks work (trivially verifiable through a stack overflow)

- review the myproc() implementation.

# Memory
- remove cr4 references from process-specific code.
- communicate that cr3 is usually the *address bits*, since
the register values holds more than just that.
- have each process track their own address space (maybe this is as simple as
    just clearing the page table entries and flushing the TLB? paging should
    create new pages)

    oh, actually we cant do this since we dont have on-demand paging yet. we'd have to
    split this into a physical memory allocator and a page fault handler interrupt first.
    after that, we can just clear the pte's and let page faults do their thing.
- consider moving type accessor functions in paging files (wrapper structs around register/int values) into macros in order to force inlining instead of relying on the compiler.
- underline or no underline for the direct type for paging type-safe values? could help with clarity, it's not directly obvious from outside the file/contract that the contracted version (pte_t vs page_table_entry_t) is the correct/type-safe value to use. double underline would make this obvious. currently, x86_64 assumes the callee will know the convention, while i386 uses the double underline/obv private-field convention.
    case against private convention:
        * multiple arguments require one of these underlying types (switch_page_directory() in i386, )

# Volatile patch series
New patch series: removing all (unnecessary) traces of volatile.
See: https://docs.kernel.org/process/volatile-considered-harmful.html

# Refactor drivers
- move code in drivers/ that doesn't drive anything into common/ utilities/ or some other place.
- Related, there's probably lots of dead code within those directories and they should be consolidated
and cleaned up.

# Refactor
* lock.{c,h} should be moved out of scheduler (out of kernel core) and into include/kernel, since
it's a publicly facing lock interface.
- consolidate x86_64 and i386 memory/paging.h within asm/
- type-guard paging in i386 like 64-bit
- fix guards in rsdp by consolidating into asm/ to define a single contract, guaranteed by both
architectures.

# Timer
- give up the CPU instead of busy-waiting for sleep. Since we work with a single-processor scheduler, we don't have to conform to strict burst cycles like the linux kernel uses (see/google dont yield in linux kernel)

# Performance
- check to make sure that the framebuffer region is mapped as cachable
- we don't read back from the framebuffer, so we can use "non-temporal stores". read up on
    movntdq/movntps and the likes.
- lk
